            <div xml:id="d979">

                <pb facs="0911" n="903" sameAs="frus:frus1941v03/pg903"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/15491: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-09-30T16:00:00+02:00">September 30, 1941—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:16 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>364. I have several times reported the belief of Turkish officials that Turkey
                    need fear no attack by Germany at any rate until spring. This has now developed
                    into a conviction (which within the past few days both Foreign Minister and
                    Secretary General have <pb facs="0912" n="904" xml:id="pg904"/> stated
                    categorically to British Ambassador, and leading staff officers to our Military
                    Attaché) that such an attack is to be definitely expected next spring.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
